l doesn't support that syntax. So the user needs to adjust the approach. Instead of trying to get the pod names and then fetch logs in one command, they should first list the Jaeger pods to get their names and then retrieve each log individually. \n\nFirst, they should run `kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger` to find the Jaeger pod names. Then, for each pod name, they can use `kubectl logs` to get the logs. But since the tool only allows read-only commands and the user can't use command substitution, they need to do this step by step. \n\nSo the next step is to get the list of Jaeger pods. Once they have the pod names, they can then fetch each log separately. This method avoids the syntax error and allows them to check the Jaeger logs for any errors related to tracing or metric collection. If there are errors in the Jaeger logs, that could explain why the metrics and traces aren't showing up, indicating a misconfiguration or a problem with the tracing service itself.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b22f4c1eb4c59b6d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 349, 'prompt_tokens': 9710, 'total_tokens': 10059, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff06-5798-7132-b1ec-a8a8aa33a705-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b22f4c1eb4c59b6d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9710, 'output_tokens': 349, 'total_tokens': 10059}), ToolMessage(content='', id='26610df0-4d34-4ab6-980e-ab8