t support command substitution, I need to split it into two steps. First, get the pod names, then log each one. \n\nSo the next step is to run kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger to get the pod names. Then, for each pod name, run kubectl logs. But since the user can only make one tool call at a time, I need to first get the pod names. Let me check if that\'s possible.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The previous attempt to use command substitution failed. To retrieve Jaeger logs, I need to first list the Jaeger pods in the namespace, then fetch their logs individually. This avoids unsupported syntax and ensures compatibility with the tool\'s constraints.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 605, 'prompt_tokens': 8723, 'total_tokens': 9328, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff06-3a05-7f82-85df-a03979a8d195-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8723, 'output_tokens': 605, 'total_tokens': 9328}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d8ec71b5-c300-4382-a808-51085a0e7e9c'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a Kubernetes cluster with a train ticket booking system. They've already checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems to be running. But then they looked into metrics and traces, which didn't return any data. Now they're trying to get Jaeger logs to see if there's a problem with the tracing setup.\n\nThe previous attempt to use command substitution in the kubectl command failed because the tool doesn't support that syntax. So the user needs to adjust the approach. Instead of trying to get the pod names and then fetch logs in one command, they should first list the Jaeger pods to get their names and then retrieve each log individually. \n\nFirst, they should run `kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger` to find the Jaeger pod names. Then, for each pod name, they can use `kubectl logs` to get the logs. But since the tool only allows read-only commands and the user can't use command substitution, they need to do this step by step. \n\nSo the next step is to get the list of Jaeger pods. Once they have the pod names, they can then fetch each log separately. This method avoids the syntax error and allows them to check the Jaeger logs for any errors related to tracing or metric collection. If there are errors in the Jaeger logs, that could explain why the metrics and traces aren't showing up, indicating a misconfiguration or a problem with the tracing service itself.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b22f4c1eb4c59b6d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 349, 'prompt_tokens': 9710, 'total_tokens': 10059, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff06-5798-7132-b1ec-a8a8aa33a705-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n train-ticket -l app=jaeger'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b22f4c1eb4c59b6d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9710, 'output_tokens': 349, 'total_tokens': 10059}), ToolMessage(content='', id='26610df0-4d34-4ab6-980e-ab