ce"}\n2026-03-18T03:36:12.864Z\tinfo\tfile/filepath_sync.go:74\twatching filepath: ./etc/flagd/flags.yaml\t{"component": "sync", "sync": "filepath"}\n', id='d52235f9-4022-4122-8757-addf79aea9ce', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b413269262893e39'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='443de4dc-ae14-4ee4-ab4c-5e3adfa109d0'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose a train ticket booking system with 41 microservices. They\'ve checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems healthy. But when they tried to get metrics and traces, there were no results. Then they checked the Jaeger pods and deployments, but none were found. The logs for the flagd service didn\'t show any errors related to Jaeger or metrics.\n\nHmm, so the main issue seems to be that the monitoring tools like Prometheus and Jaeger aren\'t set up correctly. The absence of Jaeger pods and deployments means that tracing isn\'t happening, which explains why there are no traces. Also, the metrics query returned nothing, which could be because Prometheus isn\'t scraping the necessary endpoints.\n\nWait, the user mentioned that the application is in the \'train-ticket\' namespace. Maybe the monitoring components are either not deployed in that namespace or there\'s a misconfiguration in the service discovery. The flagd service logs don\'t show any issues, so maybe it\'s not the problem. \n\nI should check if there are any other services or deployments related to Prometheus or Jaeger in the namespace. The user might have missed them. Let me think about the tools available. The \'get_services\' tool from Grafana could list all services, but the user hasn\'t used it yet. Alternatively, using \'kubectl get all -n train-ticket\' might show any missing components. But the user can only use the provided tools. \n\nWait, the \'get_services\' tool retrieves service names from Grafana, which might include the monitoring services. Let me try that. If Grafana lists the monitoring services, but they aren\'t present in the cluster, that\'s a problem. Alternatively, maybe the services are deployed but not properly configured. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the ConfigMaps or Secrets for the monitoring configurations. But the available tools don\'t include that. So the next step is to use \'get_services\' to see if the monitoring services are listed in Grafana, which might indicate they\'re supposed to be there but aren\'t running. If they aren\'t listed, that\'s a sign of a misconfiguration. \n\nSo the next tool to use is \'get_services\' to check if the monitoring services are present in Grafana, which would help confirm if they\'re supposed to be there but aren\'t running. If they are listed, then the issue is with the deployments or pods. If not, maybe the monitoring isn\'t set up correctly at all.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_services  \nreasoning: The absence of Jaeger and Prometheus components suggests a misconfiguration or missing monitoring setup. Checking the list of services from Grafana will confirm if monitoring services (e.g., Prometheus, Jaeger) are expected to be present but are missing from the cluster. This will help identify if the monitoring stack is improperly configured or not deployed.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "get_services"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 607, 'prompt_tokens': 9893, 'total_tokens': 10500, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff06-de65-7552-881f-cab5dc6c40bc-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9893, 'output_tokens': 607, 'total_tokens': 10500}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0953d4c7-7e2e-4404-bb74-1c21ef59959f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with a microservice application called Train Ticket. They've already checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems to be running normally. But there's a problem with metrics and traces not being available. \n\nFirst, they tried using Prometheus to get HTTP request rates but got an empty result. Then they checked the dependency graph via Jaeger, which also came back empty. They even looked into the Jaeger logs but found no pods, indicating that the Jaeger service might not be running.\n\nThe user then tried to check the logs of the 'flagd' service, which is part of the application, but the logs didn't show any errors related to monitoring. Now, they're using the 'get_services' tool from Grafana to list all available services. The response from that tool is empty, which suggests that either the Grafana instance isn't properly configured, or the services aren't being scraped correctly.\n\nWait, the 'get_services' tool is supposed to retrieve service names from Grafana. If it returns an empty list, that might mean that the Grafana instance isn't set up to display those services, or the services aren't being monitored at all. Since the user is using Grafana for monitoring, this could indicate that the Prometheus or Jaeger services aren't properly integrated with Grafana, leading to missing data.\n\nBut the user's main issue is that they can't get metrics or traces. The absence of Jaeger pods and the empty Grafana service list points to a possible misconfiguration in the monitoring stack. The root cause might be that the monitoring services (Prometheus, Jaeger) aren't deployed or configured correctly, leading to no data being collected. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the services are deployed but not exposed properly, so Grafana can't access them. Or the scraping rules in Prometheus are incorrect, leading to no metrics being pulled. The user needs to check the deployment status of Prometheus and Jaeger, their configurations, and ensure they're properly set up to collect metrics and traces. Also, verifying the Grafana dashboards to see if they're correctly linked to the Prometheus data source would be important.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9d5d3fb6c6576ebb', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 470, 'prompt_tokens': 10884, 'total_tokens': 11354, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff06-fc92-7a73-a98e-a329beb62577-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9d5d3fb6c6576ebb', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10884, 'output_tokens': 470, 'total_tokens': 11354}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'jaeger-all-in-one\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='83479452-ab45-4e53-be37-303cd82804bc', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9d5d3fb6c6576ebb'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f3eaab81-f33e-4109-855b-7bd2f451d63b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in a train ticket booking system with 41 microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the pods, deployments, and services, and everything seems to be running normally. But there\'s a problem with missing metrics and traces.\n\nFirst, they checked the deployments and pods, and all the microservices are in a healthy state. Then they tried querying Prometheus for metrics but got empty results. The same happened when they checked the traces with Jaeger. The dependency graph also came back empty. \n\nNext, they tried to get logs for Jaeger but found no pods or deployments for Jaeger. The logs for the flagd service didn\'t show any errors related to monitoring. Then, when they checked Grafana\'s services, they only saw \'jaeger-all-in-one\' listed. \n\nWait, the user mentioned that the application belongs to the \'train-ticket\' namespace. The \'jaeger-all-in-one\' service might be part of the Jaeger deployment. But earlier commands to check for Jaeger deployments or pods didn\'t find anything. That\'s confusing. Maybe the Jaeger service is deployed but not properly set up, or there\'s a label mismatch.\n\nThe user\'s last step was to check Grafana\'s services, which returned \'jaeger-all-in-one\'. That suggests that Jaeger is supposed to be part of the monitoring stack. But when they tried to get Jaeger pods or deployments, there were none. That inconsistency indicates a possible misconfiguration or deployment issue with Jaeger.\n\nSince the metrics and traces are missing, and the Jaeger service isn\'t showing up in the cluster, the root cause might be that the Jaeger deployment is not running correctly. The \'jaeger-all-in-one\' service might be referencing a deployment that\'s not present, or the service is misconfigured. Without Jaeger, the traces aren\'t being collected, and without Prometheus, the metrics aren\'t being scraped. \n\nThe next logical step is to check the status of the \'jaeger-all-in-one\' service. If the service exists but the pods aren\'t running, that\'s a problem. They should check the deployment for \'jaeger-all-in-one\' to see if it\'s deployed and healthy. If the deployment isn\'t there, that explains why there are no Jaeger pods. \n\nSo, the user should use the \'kubectl get deployments\' command in the \'train-ticket\' namespace to check if \'jaeger-all-in-one\' is deployed. If it\'s not, then the monitoring stack isn\'t set up correctly. If it is, but the pods are not running, there might be an issue with the deployment configuration or resource limits. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is misconfigured to point to a non-existent deployment. Checking the service\'s YAML definition could help. But since the user can only use the available tools, the next best step is to check the deployments for \'jaeger-all-in-one\' to confirm its status.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `get_services` result shows "jaeger-all-in-one" is listed in Grafana, but earlier checks found no Jaeger deployments or pods. This inconsistency suggests a misconfiguration or missing deployment for Jaeger. To resolve this, I will check if the `jaeger-all-in-one` deployment exists and is healthy in the namespace.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get deployments -n train-ticket jaeger-all-in-one"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 714, 'prompt_tokens': 10079, 'total_tokens': 10793, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff07-157e-7780-b6ec-8d5392858b3e-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10079, 'output_tokens': 714, 'total_tokens': 10793}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9ae98850-fae3-4f95-878b-56edb5968e37'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a Kubernetes cluster running a microservice-based train ticket booking system. They\'ve already checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems to be running normally. But there\'s a problem with missing metrics and traces, which is causing them to troubleshoot.\n\nFirst, they checked the services using `get_services` and found "jaeger-all-in-one" listed in Grafana. However, when they tried to check for Jaeger deployments or pods, there were none. That\'s a contradiction because if the service is listed in Grafana, there should be a corresponding deployment or pod. \n\nThe user then tried to check the deployment `jaeger-all-in-one` and got an error, which means the deployment doesn\'t exist. This indicates that the Jaeger service might not be properly deployed in the cluster. The absence of Jaeger pods and deployments explains why there are no traces available, as Jaeger is responsible for distributed tracing.\n\nSince the deployment isn\'t found, the next step is to check if the service is actually deployed. The user might have a typo in the service name, or the deployment might be under a different name. Alternatively, the service could be misconfigured, leading to it not being recognized by Kubernetes.\n\nThe root cause here seems to be the missing Jaeger deployment. Without Jaeger, the cluster can\'t collect traces, leading to the absence of trace data. The user should verify the deployment name and ensure that Jaeger is correctly deployed. If it\'s not deployed, they need to set it up properly. This would resolve the issue with missing traces and metrics, as Jaeger would then be able to collect the necessary data.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n train-ticket jaeger-all-in-one"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93303f8640d850eb', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 391, 'prompt_tokens': 11087, 'total_tokens': 11478, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff07-38ad-7490-b374-add5a386be49-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n train-ticket jaeger-all-in-one'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93303f8640d850eb', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11087, 'output_tokens': 391, 'total_tokens': 11478}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (NotFound): deployments.apps "jaeger-all-in-one" not found\n', id='5fc46915-a3cd-4b20-87e8-a4a631e081a0', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-93303f8640d850eb'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d38a496a-346f-4e4e-b5dc-659bc4b9bf22'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their microservice application called Train Ticket. They\'ve already checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems to be running fine. But when they tried to get metrics and traces, there were no results. Then they checked the dependency graph, which also came back empty. \n\nThey looked into the Jaeger logs but found no pods or deployments for Jaeger. The get_services command in Grafana showed "jaeger-all-in-one" as a service, but when they tried to check the deployment, it wasn\'t found. That\'s confusing because if the service exists, there should be a corresponding deployment.\n\nWait, maybe the Jaeger service is not properly set up. The service might be pointing to a non-existent deployment. Or perhaps the deployment was never created. The user might have a misconfiguration where the service is defined but the underlying pods aren\'t running. \n\nSince the deployment isn\'t found, the next step is to check if the service is actually present. They already used get_services and saw "jaeger-all-in-one" listed. But if the deployment isn\'t there, the service might be orphaned. Alternatively, maybe the service is using a different name or namespace. \n\nThe user should verify the service definition to see if it\'s correctly referencing the deployment. They could check the service\'s YAML to see the target port and selector. If the service is pointing to a deployment that doesn\'t exist, that\'s the root cause. \n\nAnother angle: maybe the Jaeger service is supposed to be part of a different namespace, but the user is checking the wrong namespace. The namespace for the application is train-ticket, but perhaps the Jaeger service is in another namespace. \n\nAlternatively, the user might have a typo in the service name. They mentioned "jaeger-all-in-one" in Grafana, but maybe the actual service name is different. They should check the exact name of the service in the cluster. \n\nSince the deployment isn\'t found, the service can\'t be properly monitored. The root cause is likely a missing or misconfigured Jaeger deployment, leading to no traces and metrics. The user needs to ensure that the Jaeger deployment exists and is correctly set up to match the service\'s selector.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `jaeger-all-in-one` service exists in Grafana but no deployment is found, indicating a misconfiguration or missing deployment. To confirm, I will check if the service is properly linked to a deployment and verify its configuration.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get service jaeger-all-in-one -n train-ticket -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 558, 'prompt_tokens': 10304, 'total_tokens': 10862, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff07-4f0b-7232-9db6-bedbe41ed464-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10304, 'output_tokens': 558, 'total_tokens': 10862}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5bc2d011-e827-4322-964b-ffb04540b71b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their microservices application called Train Ticket. They\'ve already checked the pods, deployments, and services, and everything seems to be running fine. But there\'s a problem with metrics and traces not being available.\n\nFirst, they checked the services using `kubectl get services -n train-ticket` and saw that there\'s a service named `jaeger` and others. Then they tried to get deployments and pods, which all showed as healthy. But when they tried to get metrics from Prometheus, there were no results. They also tried tracing with Jaeger but got empty results. The dependency graph also came back empty.\n\nThen they tried to check the logs for Jaeger pods but found none. The `get_services` command from Grafana showed "jaeger-all-in-one" as a service, but when they tried to check the deployment, it wasn\'t found. The service might be misconfigured or not properly linked to a deployment.\n\nNow, the user is trying to get the service YAML for `jaeger-all-in-one` to see its configuration. If the service exists but there\'s no deployment, that could explain why there are no pods or logs. The service might be pointing to a non-existent deployment, leading to no actual running pods. This would cause the metrics and traces to not be collected because Jaeger isn\'t running.\n\nBy checking the service\'s YAML, they can see the spec, like the selector and endpoints. If the service is not properly linked to any pods, that\'s a clear sign of a misconfiguration. The next step is to verify the service\'s configuration to confirm if it\'s set up correctly or if there\'s a missing deployment.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service jaeger-all-in-one -n train-ticket -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-874e3fbe46e0772c', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 390, 'prompt_tokens': 11293, 'total_tokens': 11683, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff07-6aaa-7063-a56f-91d523d171b3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service jaeger-all-in-one -n train-ticket -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-874e3fbe46e0772c', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11293, 'output_tokens': 390, 'total_tokens': 11683}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (NotFound): services "jaeger-all-in-one" not found\n', id='01fe94ee-2184-4659-a677-f37d66838a49', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-874e3fbe46e0772c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e7bea338-25dc-4b75-87bc-e7f8030d9469'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their train ticket booking system, which has 41 microservices. They\'ve been checking the Kubernetes cluster using various tools like kubectl, Prometheus, and Jaeger. \n\nFirst, they listed all the services in the \'train-ticket\' namespace and found several services like jaeger, nacos, rabbitmq, etc. Then they checked deployments and pods, which all seemed to be in a healthy state. They also tried to get metrics and traces but didn\'t find any data. \n\nThe key issue seems to be with Jaeger. The user tried to get logs for Jaeger pods but found none, and when they checked for deployments and services related to Jaeger, they got "not found" errors. The Grafana service list shows "jaeger-all-in-one" as a service, but it\'s not present in the cluster. \n\nSo, the problem is likely that the Jaeger service isn\'t properly deployed. The service might be misconfigured or the deployment isn\'t set up correctly. Since the service isn\'t found, it\'s possible that the deployment for Jaeger isn\'t running, leading to no traces and metrics. \n\nThe next step is to check the ConfigMap for Jaeger to see if there\'s any configuration that might be causing the issue. If the ConfigMap is missing or misconfigured, it could prevent Jaeger from starting. By checking the ConfigMap, they can verify if the necessary settings are present and correct. If the ConfigMap is missing, that would explain why the service and deployment aren\'t found, leading to the lack of tracing data.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The absence of the `jaeger-all-in-one` service and deployment suggests a misconfiguration or missing resource. To investigate further, I will check if a ConfigMap named `jaeger-all-in-one` exists, as it might store critical configuration settings for Jaeger. This will help determine if the service is improperly configured or missing entirely.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap jaeger-all-in-one -n train-ticket"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 449, 'prompt_tokens': 10512, 'total_tokens': 10961, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff07-8134-76d2-a450-2950b3285fe7-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10512, 'output_tokens': 449, 'total_tokens': 10961}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8cb6d4e8-8776-4793-8743-bf8e598ef93b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their microservices application called Train Ticket. They've gone through several steps already, checking pods, deployments, metrics, traces, and dependencies. So far, everything seems healthy at the pod and deployment levels. But there's a problem with monitoring—no metrics or traces are being collected.\n\nFirst, they checked the services in the namespace and found a service called jaeger-all-in-one listed in Grafana. But when they tried to get the deployment or service details, it wasn't found. That's confusing because the service should exist if it's listed in Grafana. Then they tried checking for a ConfigMap named jaeger-all-in-one, which might hold configuration for Jaeger. If that's not found either, it suggests that the Jaeger setup isn't properly deployed or configured.\n\nThe user's next step is to check if the ConfigMap exists. If it's not there, that could mean the Jaeger configuration wasn't applied correctly, leading to the service and deployment not being created. Without the ConfigMap, the Jaeger components might not start, resulting in no traces and possibly no metrics if other monitoring tools are similarly misconfigured.\n\nSo, the tool call to check the ConfigMap makes sense. If the ConfigMap is missing, it points to a configuration error or a deployment issue. If it's present, maybe there's another problem, like the service or deployment not being created despite the ConfigMap. Either way, this step helps narrow down whether the issue is with the configuration or the deployment itself.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap jaeger-all-in-one -n train-ticket"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a46937fe172e8622', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 368, 'prompt_tokens': 11520, 'total_tokens': 11888, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff07-9782-7562-a673-c271c13d101e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap jaeger-all-in-one -n train-ticket'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a46937fe172e8622', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11520, 'output_tokens': 368, 'total_tokens': 11888}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (NotFound): configmaps "jaeger-all-in-one" not found\n', id='8878481c-c817-4341-acf9-e92f12470e7b', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a46937fe172e8622'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='03932421-ea03-47a1-b442-0e8eca044afb')]]
